    <article class="prose">
      <h2>What is face blurring?</h2>
      <p>Face blurring hides the faces of people in a photo so that other people cannot recognize them. The face is replaced with a soft blur, large pixels or a black shape, and the rest of the photo stays the same.</p>
      <p>Blurring is different from cropping. When you blur faces, you keep the scene, the place and what happened, and remove only the part that identifies a person.</p>

      <h2>Why use a face blur tool?</h2>
      <p>Hiding faces by hand in a photo editor is slow, and it is easy to miss one. A group photo, a street scene or a crowd at an event can have dozens of faces, some of them small and in the background. A face blur tool finds them for you in seconds and hides them all with the same style.</p>
      <p>People blur faces to protect children, bystanders and anyone who did not agree to be in a public photo. It also matters when you post photos on social media, publish news or reports, sell items online or share evidence of an incident.</p>

      <h2>How does the Face Blur tool work?</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Add a photo.</strong> Drop it on the page, paste it, or click "Choose a photo". Click "Try an example photo" to see how it works.</li>
        <li><strong>Wait for the scan.</strong> The AI looks for faces and puts a box on each face it finds. All the faces are hidden.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a style</strong>: Blur, Pixelate or Black box. Set the strength, and choose an oval or a rectangle shape.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the result.</strong> Click a box to show that face. Drag on the photo to hide anything that was missed. Hold "Hold to see original" to compare.</li>
        <li><strong>Download</strong> the new photo. It has the same format as the original: JPG, PNG or WebP.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The tool uses YuNet, a small face detection model from OpenCV. It looks at the whole photo to find large faces. In a large photo, it also looks at smaller parts of the photo, one at a time, to find faces in the background. Each box is made a little larger than the face, so it also covers the hair, ears and chin.</p>

      <h2>Is my photo safe?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Many online face blur tools upload your photo to a server to process it. This one does not. The page has no server code: face detection and blurring run entirely in your browser, and your photo never leaves your device.</p>
      <p>The face model is only 230 KB, so it loads quickly when you open the page. The download contains only the model, not your data. Read our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for the details.</p>

      <h2>What can the Face Blur tool find and hide?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Faces</strong> of people who look at the camera</li>
        <li><strong>Faces in groups and crowds</strong>, including small faces in the background of large photos</li>
        <li><strong>Many faces turned to the side</strong>, though faces that are turned far away can be missed</li>
        <li><strong>Any area you choose</strong>, such as a name badge, a license plate, a house number, a screen or a reflection</li>
        <li><strong>Location and camera data</strong>, which is not copied into the downloaded photo</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who uses a face blur tool?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Parents and schools</strong> who share photos of events without showing other people's children.</li>
        <li><strong>Journalists and bloggers</strong> who publish photos of protests, crowds or street scenes and protect the people in them.</li>
        <li><strong>Social media users</strong> who post photos with friends or strangers who did not agree to be shown.</li>
        <li><strong>Researchers</strong> who share photos from field work or studies with the participants made anonymous.</li>
        <li><strong>Sellers and landlords</strong> who post photos of items or rooms with people or family photos in the background.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone who reports an incident</strong> and wants to share a photo without showing bystanders.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Face Blur vs. a photo editor</h2>
      <p>In a photo editor, you select each face by hand and apply a blur filter. It works for one or two faces, but in a group photo people miss faces, and the blur is not always the same on each face. Some apps also only put a blur layer on top, which can be removed if you share the project file instead of a flat image.</p>
      <p>The Face Blur tool finds all the faces at once, hides them with the same style, and saves a new flat image. It is still good practice to look at the result before you share it. For faces in a video, use the <a href="/tools/video-face-blur/">Video Face Blur</a> tool. To remove location and camera data from a photo without changing it, use the <a href="/tools/metadata-stripper/">Metadata Stripper</a>.</p>

